          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A 30-year-old man presents with acute watery diarrhoea two days after completing</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A 30-year-old man presents with acute watery diarrhoea two days after completing a course of amoxicillin for a respiratory infection. Which organism should this medication history prompt you to specifically investigate?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Shigella dysenteriae</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Shigella causes dysentery through faeco-oral transmission and is unrelated to antibiotic exposure. The medication history here is the critical diagnostic clue.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Clostridioides difficile</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Recent antibiotic use is the strongest risk factor for C. difficile colitis. A thorough medication history directly changes the differential in acute diarrhoea.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Vibrio cholerae</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Cholera presents with rice-water stools in endemic or epidemic settings and is unrelated to prior antibiotic exposure.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Cryptosporidium parvum</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Cryptosporidium is primarily associated with immunocompromised patients, particularly those with HIV, not with recent antibiotic use.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The key clue is the recent antibiotic course. Clostridioides difficile is the organism most strongly linked to post-antibiotic diarrhoea."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Exactly right. Eliciting medication history is essential — recent antibiotics are the strongest risk factor for Clostridioides difficile colitis."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
