          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A patient with acute diarrhoea has a complete blood count showing leucopenia with relative lymphocytosis. Which diagnosis does this haematological pattern most strongly suggest?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Shigella dysentery</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Shigella infection typically causes leucocytosis with a neutrophilic shift due to mucosal invasion, not leucopenia.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Helminthic infection</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Helminths characteristically cause eosinophilia, not leucopenia with lymphocytosis.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Enteric fever</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Leucopenia with relative lymphocytosis is a characteristic finding in enteric (typhoid) fever, helping distinguish it from other causes of acute diarrhoea.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Cholera</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Cholera typically shows haemoconcentration from massive fluid loss, but leucopenia with relative lymphocytosis is not a characteristic feature.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Leucopenia with relative lymphocytosis is the characteristic CBC pattern of enteric (typhoid) fever. In contrast, invasive bacterial infections cause leucocytosis and helminths cause eosinophilia."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct. Leucopenia with relative lymphocytosis is a classic CBC pattern in enteric fever — a finding that guides both diagnosis and further investigation."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
