          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A patient presents with bloody, mucoid diarrhoea, fever, and tenesmus. The atten</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A patient presents with bloody, mucoid diarrhoea, fever, and tenesmus. The attending physician considers prescribing loperamide to reduce stool frequency. What is the most important concern with this decision?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Worsening of dehydration due to reduced fluid losses</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Loperamide reduces stool output and would not worsen dehydration. The actual risk in dysentery is far more dangerous than fluid imbalance.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Significant drug interaction with co-prescribed antibiotics</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Loperamide does not have clinically significant interactions with antibiotics used in dysentery. The concern is about the drug's mechanism of action in an invasive infection.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Risk of toxic megacolon from slowed intestinal transit</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Antimotility agents are contraindicated in dysentery. Slowing transit in the presence of mucosal invasion allows organisms to penetrate deeper and risks toxic megacolon.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Masking fever and delaying appropriate treatment</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Loperamide acts on gut opioid receptors to slow motility; it does not suppress the febrile response. The primary concern is a life-threatening surgical emergency, not diagnostic delay.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The critical concern is toxic megacolon. Loperamide slows intestinal transit, and in dysentery this allows invasive organisms to penetrate deeper into damaged mucosa, risking perforation."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct. Antimotility agents like loperamide are absolutely contraindicated in dysentery — slowing transit risks toxic megacolon when the mucosa is already invaded."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
                "enableRetry": true,
