          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>After treating invasive intestinal amoebiasis with metronidazole, what is the ma</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>After treating invasive intestinal amoebiasis with metronidazole, what is the mandatory next step that is most commonly missed in clinical practice?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Repeating the metronidazole course for an additional week to ensure complete clearance</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>A second metronidazole course does not address luminal cyst carriage and increases the risk of adverse effects such as peripheral neuropathy.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Prescribing a luminal agent such as diloxanide furoate to eliminate cyst carriage</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Metronidazole kills tissue trophozoites but does not reliably clear cysts from the gut lumen. A luminal agent is mandatory to prevent relapse and ongoing transmission.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Adding albendazole to cover possible co-infection with helminths</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Albendazole targets helminths, not Entamoeba cysts. It is not routinely added after amoebiasis treatment unless helminthic co-infection is confirmed.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Ordering a colonoscopy within one week to confirm mucosal healing</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Routine colonoscopy is not indicated after treating uncomplicated intestinal amoebiasis. Follow-up stool microscopy is used for test of cure.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The most commonly missed step is prescribing a luminal agent (diloxanide furoate or paromomycin) after metronidazole. Metronidazole clears tissue trophozoites but not luminal cysts."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct. Following metronidazole with a luminal agent (diloxanide furoate or paromomycin) is mandatory — without it, the patient remains a cyst carrier with risk of relapse and transmission."
                }
              ],
